---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import overviewDay from "../../../assets/night-city/overview-day.png";
import overviewNight from "../../../assets/night-city/overview-night.png";
import vacuumDetails from "../../../assets/night-city/vacuum-details.png";
import ipadDesk from "../../../assets/night-city/ipad-desk.jpg";

const stack = [
  { area: "Backend", tech: "Node.js, Fastify" },
  {
    area: "Frontend",
    tech: "Plain ES5 JavaScript, HTML, CSS (no framework, no build step)",
  },
  { area: "Integration", tech: "Home Assistant REST API" },
  { area: "Hardware", tech: "First-generation iPad mini, iOS 9.3" },
  { area: "Deployment", tech: "Docker in a Proxmox VM, deployed with Coolify" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Night City dashboard | Michael Søby"
  description="A Home Assistant dashboard built to run on a first-generation iPad mini."
>
  <article class="project-detail">
    <header class="project-detail-header">
      <a href="/projects/" class="back-link">
        ← Back to projects
      </a>

      <p class="eyebrow">Personal project · Running at home</p>

      <h1>Night City dashboard</h1>

      <p class="project-lead">
        A dashboard for my apartment's Home Assistant setup, built to run on a
        first-generation iPad mini from 2012. One glance shows the lights, the
        electricity price, the vacuum and the weather.
      </p>

      <div class="project-detail-links">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/Ohshi60/night-city-dashboard"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          View source on GitHub ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <figure class="photo photo-lead">
      <Image
        src={ipadDesk}
        alt="A first-generation iPad mini in a wooden stand on a desk, running the Night City dashboard: lighting scenes, electricity prices and the vacuum's status."
        widths={[400, 800, 1200, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 864px) 100vw, 800px"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Running on a first-generation iPad mini on my desk.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="project-detail-body">
      <section>
        <h2>The constraint</h2>
        <p>
          The iPad mini tops out at iOS 9.3, so its Safari predates almost
          everything modern web development takes for granted: no arrow
          functions, <code>let</code> or <code>const</code>, no
          <code>fetch</code>, no promises. Rather than add a transpiler, the
          frontend is written directly in ES5 with
          <code>XMLHttpRequest</code>, and a check script fails if any newer
          syntax slips in.
        </p>
        <p>
          The old hardware shaped the rest of the design too. The page polls for
          state every 8 seconds instead of holding a WebSocket open, and it only
          rebuilds a card when that card's data has changed, so a tap isn't lost
          to a re-render. The whole overview fits one 1024×690 screen, and
          tapping a card opens its details in a panel that closes itself after a
          minute.
        </p>
      </section>

      <figure class="screenshot">
        <div class="screenshot-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
        </div>
        <Image
          src={overviewDay}
          alt="Night City dashboard overview: lighting scenes on the left; electricity price of 3.33 kr/kWh with a bar chart and the cheapest start times for the washing machine and dishwasher; vacuum status at 80% battery with a reminder to wipe the sensors."
          widths={[400, 680, 1020, 1360]}
          sizes="(max-width: 744px) 100vw, 680px"
        />
        <figcaption>
          The overview, sized to fit the iPad mini's screen in landscape. Demo
          data.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section>
        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <ul class="detail-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Lighting.</strong> The Home Assistant scenes for the office,
            the state of each light they control, and an "all lights off"
            button.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Electricity prices.</strong> The current price per kWh
            including tariffs and VAT, the coming hours as a chart, and the
            cheapest time to start the washing machine or dishwasher.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The robot vacuum.</strong> Status, battery and consumables,
            plus the last run in plain language: rooms it skipped and why, and
            rooms that keep getting skipped.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Now playing and weather.</strong> A slim bar while something
            plays on Plex or Jellyfin, and a weather chip with the rain outlook
            in words.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <figure class="screenshot">
        <div class="screenshot-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
        </div>
        <Image
          src={vacuumDetails}
          alt="Vacuum details panel: the last run didn't finish after 67 minutes and 35 square metres, the balcony was skipped because the door was closed and has been skipped in 2 of the last 2 runs, and wear levels for the brushes, filter, wheel and sensors."
          widths={[400, 680, 1020, 1360]}
          sizes="(max-width: 744px) 100vw, 680px"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Vacuum details: what happened on the last run, not just error codes.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section>
        <h2>Safe enough for the LAN</h2>
        <p>
          A dashboard that can switch the lights is still a control surface, so
          it's deliberately narrow:
        </p>
        <ul class="detail-list">
          <li>
            The Home Assistant token stays on the server and never reaches the
            browser. Even images such as media artwork are proxied through it.
          </li>
          <li>
            The browser can't make arbitrary Home Assistant calls. The server
            allows exactly two actions: activate one of the shown scenes, and
            turn all lights off.
          </li>
          <li>
            A strict Content Security Policy, and every POST must be same-origin
            and carry a custom header, which guards against cross-site requests.
          </li>
          <li>
            There's no login, so it's LAN-only by design and never exposed to
            the internet.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Demo mode and night mode</h2>
        <p>
          Demo mode serves made-up device states through the same summary code
          as live mode, so the two can't drift apart, and the dashboard can be
          developed without touching the real apartment. The screenshots on this
          page come from it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Between sunset and sunrise, the page switches to a dimmer, warmer
          theme.
        </p>
      </section>

      <figure class="screenshot">
        <div class="screenshot-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
        </div>
        <Image
          src={overviewNight}
          alt="The same overview in night mode, with dimmer, warmer colours."
          widths={[400, 680, 1020, 1360]}
          sizes="(max-width: 744px) 100vw, 680px"
        />
        <figcaption>Night mode, from sunset to sunrise.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section>
        <h2>Tech stack</h2>
        <dl class="project-facts">
          {stack.map((row) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{row.area}</dt>
              <dd>{row.tech}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
